our  million dollars Senator Cortez masso has  spent more than 20 million dollars  laxalt has spent about 20 percent of  that 4.3 million dollars  from coronavirus to water issues coming  up next on politics now County  Commissioner Jim Gibson speaks with us  about his re-election campaign and more  changes suggested for school boards  around Nevada the latest proposal from  the lieutenant governor  message  welcome back to politics now  tonight we continue our profile of the  race for Clark County Commission Now  last week we spoke with Republican Billy  Mitchell this week I sat down with the  man he is challenging for the Clark  County Commission incumbent Democrat and  chair Jim Gibson I started off asking  him about the public administrator's  office  was there anything else that you could  have done for those people uh in that  office well I think we did what we could  do for the people there  we took some administrative  responsibility just because there are  people we don't run the office we're not  the legal officer that can do the things  that need to be done although Mike is  deputized so he can function there you  know if there were anything maybe the  public administrator shouldn't even be  in elected office could we have done  something well I know one of our members  tried to do that when she was in the  legislature but I know I think it takes  a legislative action to do that let's  switch gears let's talk about the the  election itself Europe for re-election  obviously  um and let's talk about what's happened  in the last couple of years with the  County Commission the biggest challenges  you've had to deal with through covid  and everything else how would you grade  your performance getting through the  depths of the pandemic  frankly I think we worked well together  there was always going to be conflict  both internally because we have  different views about things and with  others you know the business Community  was frantic to get back open early on no  one was coming to Las Vegas so because  we're dependent upon tourism and  travelers and Airlines were shut down  and there were empty buildings it it was  something that was it didn't require a  lot of action on the part of anyone but  it was not we who were shutting down or  making determinations about  what was essential that was a  gubernatorial determination what we did  was we worked within the confines of his  emergency powers to enable a restart at  some point and we were working very hard  trying to make the case where it was  appropriate that it was time to reopen  some things and to allow other things to  begin to get ready to be open I also  want to just touch on something else  that your opponent has brought up and  you know you've had a long political  career you were the mayor in Henderson  you've you've been in politics for a  long time here there have been  accusations made with regards to ethics  campaign financing debt from when you  are the mayor of Henderson sort of  lingering Financial fiscal issues that  are that are sort of being brought up to  be critical of your performance how do  you tell voters that you're the best  Steward of their money that when you're  in office you're the best person to  represent them and get the county moving  forward well first of all in the city of  Henderson we operated on a really tight  budget and always always were operating  within the parameters that were assigned  so there was no debt you know when there  was a loose comment that wasn't intended  to convey the message that was conveyed  when the mayor who succeeded me said I  inherited a 90 million dollar deficit  well there are no deficit what he  inherited was a circumstance a session  that was rolling in as I was leaving  office and so we had already done a  voluntary retirement program and we'd  saved considerable money on payroll we  were cutting back in areas that were  really important keeping we had even  debate about do we leave rec centers  open or do we close no we need those  more than anything they're paid for  people don't have to pay to go there but  we were having those debates as I left  office I want to talk about the future  we talked a little bit maybe too much  about the past here the future here one  of the major issues for Clark County is  water you and I have talked about this  before if you're re-elected next four  years what can Clark County do you're  talking about getting a seat at the  table what can Clark County do to try  and address what's happening at Lake  Mead Beyond issues with Turf and pools  and so forth well keep in mind that four  percent of the water that comes out of  Lake Mead comes to Nevada four percent  so what what we've been doing is  preparing ourselves for whatever the  next iterate duration of challenges on  the river may be my opponent thinks that  the reason the lake is going down is  that California is taking too much water  out it wouldn't matter if California  were doing our program  there would still not be enough water in  the lake because there's a drought  we have a full interview with Jim Gibson  and the interview we did with his  opponent Billy Mitchell at 8newsnow.com  next on politics now battling for  control of the Senate CBS news's Lilia  Luciano looks inside to that is closely  watched Senate rates  live at 6 on 8 news now you're watching  politics now  as we've discussed Nevada is one of the  key state States in this election cycle  some of the biggest races could decide  the balance of power in Washington a new  CBS new poll news poll shows Democratic  senator Catherine Cortez masto is still  locked in a very close re-election race  with Republican Adam laxalt Lele Luciano  followed both of them on the campaign  Trail  as you know this is an incredibly tight  race and one where the voters here in  Nevada your viewers  where the stakes are it might determine  who controls the Senate 8 out of 10  voters in our Battleground tracker  realized said that they understand that  they are voting in this election not  just for their Senator but who takes  control of the senate in Washington  foreign  states in the Nevada midterm races are  high and odds are Latinos will decide  making up 20 percent of the state's  electorate Governor House and Senate  candidates are reaching out to secure  votes among the fastest growing minority  the more powerful and effective in a  list Culinary Union knocking on more  than a million doors ahead of this  election why are you doing this I'm  doing this because I think the one of  the issues that I'm most concerned about  is the housing right now I wish um it  wasn't so expensive plan to take away  the freedom this year the union endorsed  incumbent Democratic senator Catherine  Cortes masto who made history as the  first Latina ever in the Senate but the  CBS News Battleground tracker shows her  statistically tied with her opponent  former Nevada attorney general Adam  laxalt so even after hours Cortez masto  is making the rounds my focus is on  nevadans that's always been my priority  whether it's a small business owner like  you have here or it's families that are  one every opportunity to succeed for  themselves and their kids that's what  it's about for me that's the work that I  get to do in the Senate every day for a  state hit hard by covet closures with  inflation and gas prices among the  highest in the country voting in this  election is about nothing more than  making ends meet says laxalt's advisor  Jesus Marquez Latinos in the past used  to think that okay we need to vote for a  Latina or a Latino but now it doesn't  matter if whether it's Latino or not as  long as it takes care of the problems I  can only tell you yeah that what I hear  from Nevada and it it really is the same  story for all of us which is that  American Dream you know it is the  opportunity to succeed but her opponent  has a fierce finger pointed at Democrats  as to why that dream is Out Of Reach  yeah tell them look outside and  everything their experience is a direct  result of Joe Biden and Senator  Catherine Cortez masto all the pain  they're feeling today at the pump at the  grocery store in their family budgets is  a direct result of policy they're  killing the American dream there's work  that I've been able to do with in  conjunction with them to start figuring  how we lower those costs because we know  it's squeezing families I think the  inflation reduction Act was a great  first start but I do think absolutely  there's more that we can do together you  know it all comes down to who can  convince voters here in this state that  their Solutions are the ones that are  going to impact their finances  towards housing inflation is the cost of  living in here is well known gas prices  and the prices of you know everyday  products are higher than in other parts  of the country  [Music]  and that was Lily Luciano reporting more  politics  Toyota Let's Go Places you're watching  politics now  lieutenant governor Lisa Cano Burkhead  is suggesting a new bill to change the  makeup of our school boards her proposal  would add four appointed people to the  Clark County School Board for example a  president would be appointed by the  Clark County Commission the cities of  Las Vegas Henderson and North Las Vegas  would each get to a point one member as  well Cano Burkhead says it will be one  of her Bill draft requests in the 2023  session now she is a Democrat facing  reelection right now against Republican  Stavros Anthony  a few weeks ago we reported Nevada's  largest teachers union is not endorsing  a candidate in the gubernatorial race  that's the Clark County Education  Association which decided not to back  anyone however other educator unions  across the state are putting their  weight behind Governor sisalak that's  the Nevada State Education Association  School support staff they are endorsing  the incumbent ccean nsea are often at  loggerheads and this